``` Introduction

`@` unpacks an instance's members into a sibling scope, so its fields are reachable without an `instance.` prefix. A sibling scope is only checked as a fallback, after a scope's own declarations -- it doesn't override anything already reachable, it just adds another place to look.
```
Raft {
	length := 0
	crew := 0
}

r := Raft()
r.length = 12
r.crew = 4
@ += r
@puts "length: `length`"   # length: 12 -- reached via the sibling scope, no r. prefix needed
@ -= r


``` Auto-unpack in function parameters

`@param` in a function's signature unpacks that argument into a sibling scope for the duration of the call -- the body reads its members directly:
```
Vector {
	x := 0
	y := 0
}

magnitude { @vec;
	(x ** 2 + y ** 2).sqrt()
}

v := Vector()
v.x = 3
v.y = 4
@puts "magnitude(v): `magnitude(v)`"   # magnitude(v): 5.0


``` Manual sibling scope control

`@ += instance` and `@ -= instance` do the same unpacking by hand, in any scope, not just a function parameter list -- useful when the instance to unpack isn't known until the body runs:
```
Island {
	name := 'unknown',
}

island := Island()
island.name = 'The Island'

@ += island
@puts "name: `name`"   # name: The Island
@ -= island
# @puts name would now raise Ore::Undeclared_Identifier -- island was removed from the sibling scope


``` Unpacked instances stay visible to later functions, even nested ones

Once unpacked, an instance's members stay reachable from any function declared afterward in that scope -- including one nested inside another function -- because sibling scopes are just another place identifier lookup checks, not a one-time substitution:
```
Camp {
	survivors := 0
	water := 0

	new { survivors, water;
		./survivors = survivors
		./water = water
	}
}

ration_report {;
	c := Camp(12, 40)
	@ += c

	summarize {;
		"`survivors` survivors, `water` units of water"
	}

	summarize()
}

@puts "ration_report(): `ration_report()`"   # ration_report(): 12 survivors, 40 units of water
